@title Choosing Ardent Bio's next home
@subtitle Three buildings for 96 people, about 24,000 sq ft with roughly a third wet lab. Leadership decides on 2026-11-13.
@date 2026-10-17

Our current lease ends on 2027-03-31. All three buildings can take us before then. The choice is between paying more for a lab-ready building, paying up front to finish a cheaper one, or splitting the team across two sites.

## Options

table = ,Harbor Point,Mill District,Northgate Park; Base rent per sq ft per year,$58,$46,$38; Lease term,10 years,7 years,10 years; Lab space,Lab-ready now,Partly lab-ready,None; Extra cost to us,None,"About $1.1M fit-out",Separate lab lease nearby; Median employee commute,24 min,31 min,42 min; Move-in date,2027-02-01,2027-03-01,2027-01-15

## Annual rent

data rent = building,annual base rent; Harbor Point,1392000; Mill District,1104000; Northgate Park,912000
chart bar rent unit=$ labels title="Annual base rent at 24,000 sq ft"

Harbor Point costs $288,000 a year more in base rent than Mill District. Mill District saves that money every year, but the $1.1M fit-out uses up the first four years or so of those savings.

! These are base rent only. Mill District's figure leaves out the $1.1M fit-out, and Northgate Park's leaves out the separate lab lease we would need.

## Pros and cons

### Harbor Point
- Pro: lab-ready now, so no fit-out project and no fit-out risk.
- Pro: shortest commute for most of the team, at 24 minutes.
- Pro: move-in on 2027-02-01 leaves two months of slack before the lease ends.
- Con: the most expensive base rent by far, $1,392,000 a year.
- Con: a 10-year commitment at the highest price.

### Mill District
- Pro: $288,000 a year less in base rent than Harbor Point.
- Pro: one site for the whole team, with a 31-minute median commute.
- Con: we pay about $1.1M to finish the lab space, and fit-outs can run late or over budget.
- Con: move-in on 2027-03-01 leaves only one month before the current lease ends.
- Con: the 7-year term means we spend that $1.1M on a building we may have to leave or renegotiate sooner.

### Northgate Park
- Pro: the cheapest base rent, $912,000 a year.
- Pro: the earliest move-in, 2027-01-15.
- Con: no lab space, so lab staff work in a second building and the team is split across two sites.
- Con: a second lab lease adds cost that is not in the figure above.
- Con: the longest commute, at 42 minutes.

## Recommendation

**We lean toward Mill District.** It keeps the whole team in one place with lab space, and costs $288,000 a year less in base rent than Harbor Point. Northgate Park is cheaper on paper, but splitting the team across two sites works against how we do science.

The downsides are real and the leadership team should weigh them openly:
- The $1.1M fit-out is money we spend before we save anything, and it has to finish on time.
- A one-month gap between move-in and lease end gives us little room if the fit-out slips.
- A 7-year term is shorter than the other two, so we may face a move or a rent renegotiation sooner.

!! If the fit-out cannot be firmly scheduled to finish by 2027-03-01, Harbor Point becomes the safer choice despite the higher rent.
